36:[
U('36a','Device interfaces, polling, interrupts and DMA','36.1–36.5',[410,415],
'When you copy a large file, the CPU can run your editor while the storage device transfers data. What work actually remains for the CPU?',
['Which registers communicate commands and completion?','How does the CPU learn the operation is finished?'],
['The canonical device exposes status, command and data registers. A polling driver waits for ready status, supplies data and command, and checks completion. Interrupts let a CPU do other work while the device operates; polling can be cheaper for extremely short operations. DMA moves a block between device and memory after CPU setup, with completion notification. Notification and transfer are separate mechanisms.'],
[['Polling versus interrupts','Device busy for 100 units {given model}.','Polling once per unit uses 100 checks {CPU repeatedly observes status}.','Interrupt-driven driver initiates operation, executes other work, handles completion {no repeated busy checks}.','Interrupt processing also costs time {not universally faster}.'],['DMA write','Driver prepares a memory buffer and transfer descriptor {address and length}.','Device/DMA engine transfers the bytes {CPU need not copy each word}.','CPU runs other work during transfer {overlap possible}.','Completion interrupt informs driver; driver checks result and releases buffer when safe {lifetime and status}.']],
['Read the canonical protocol and redraw the register interactions.','Use Activity Monitor during a normal bounded file copy and note CPU and disk activity.','Explain why utilization alone cannot prove whether a particular driver uses interrupts or DMA.'],
[['Which mechanism repeatedly reads device status?','polling','Map repeated status checks in Example 1.'],['Which mechanism moves data without CPU copying each word?','dma|direct memory access','Example 2 separates setup and transfer.']],
[['Does DMA eliminate all CPU involvement?','no','The driver still configures and handles completion.'],['Is an interrupt itself the mechanism that transfers a data block?','no','Notification is separate from movement.']],
['Draw CPU/device timelines for polling, interrupt-driven programmed I/O and DMA.','Label setup, data movement and completion on each.','Hence compare their CPU cost for short and long transfers.'],
['Correct register/protocol roles and notification vs data movement.','Explain concurrent CPU work and transfer-buffer lifetime.','Compare costs without claiming one technique always wins.'],
[['Status register tells driver?','Device state','Virtual page size','Thread ticket','File name'],['Interrupt can reduce?','Busy polling CPU work','All device latency','Every memory transfer','Disk capacity'],['DMA requires?','CPU setup of transfer','No buffer','No completion handling','A new process per byte'],['Polling can be sensible when?','Expected wait is very short','CPU must never read status','No device exists','All requests are always slow'],['Completion notification and data movement are?','Distinct mechanisms','Identical operations','Always both polling','Only filesystem operations']]),
U('36b','Device interaction, drivers and IDE case study','36.6–36.10',[415,422],
'A filesystem needs to issue a block read without knowing the exact storage controller registers. Which abstraction makes that possible?',
['How can the driver address device registers?','What details should the upper filesystem layer not need?'],
['Port I/O uses special instructions to access device registers; memory-mapped I/O uses addresses mapped to device registers. Device drivers translate an OS-facing interface into device-specific commands and handle completion/errors. The textbook IDE driver is a historical example of queuing, issuing a request and completing it in an interrupt handler. MMIO requires architecture/device-specific access and ordering rules.'],
[['Read through layers','Application issues read(fd,buf,n) {system call}.','Filesystem resolves file position to logical block {filesystem abstraction}.','Block layer submits request to driver {device interface}.','Driver programs controller and processes completion {device details}.','Bytes are returned through upper layers {application need not know register layout}.'],['Queued IDE-style request','Queue contains A then B; A issued while B waits {one outstanding request in simple model}.','Device finishes A and raises interrupt {completion notification}.','Driver obtains status/data, completes A, wakes waiter {result delivered}.','Driver starts B {next queue entry}.']],
['Read the IDE case study and label request submission versus interrupt handler.','Trace a small read through application, filesystem, block layer and driver on paper.','Compare historical HDD driver assumptions with the abstract interface used for your Mac SSD; do not infer exact Apple driver internals.'],
[['What component translates generic block requests to controller commands?','device driver|driver','Map Example 1.'],['What technique accesses registers through mapped addresses?','mmio|memory-mapped i/o|memory mapped i/o','Device register access resembles memory addressing.']],
[['A driver completes A and queue still contains B. What should the simple queue driver issue next?','b','Advance the request queue.'],['Does MMIO mean device register reads behave exactly like ordinary cacheable RAM?','no','Ordering and device side effects differ.']],
['Trace two queued disk reads through a generic block interface and IDE-style driver.','Identify synchronization, buffer ownership and errors at submission/completion.','Hence explain how changing the hardware need not change the file API.'],
['Separate layers and device-specific register access.','Track outstanding requests and completion/error handling.','State historical model vs modern hardware distinction.'],
[['Driver purpose?','Translate OS requests to device operations','Replace every application','Choose CPU quantum only','Store user stack'],['MMIO means?','Device registers mapped into address space','File contents stored as CPU registers','No memory addresses','Always DMA'],['Port I/O generally uses?','Special I/O instructions','Only file names','Page replacement','Ordinary malloc'],['Interrupt handler can?','Complete request and wake waiter','Always create a new file','Ignore controller status','Replace application executable'],['Filesystem block layer should hide?','Device-specific command/register details','All errors','File length','Every allocation decision']])
],
